## Problem

Some terminals (macOS Terminal.app default config, certain SSH setups, Linux consoles without TIOCLINUX support) do not distinguish `Enter` from `shift+Enter` — both arrive as `\r`. Without a fallback, users on those terminals cannot insert a literal newline and are forced to submit prematurely.

Upstream's editor has a workaround: when the user submits with Enter and the **character before the cursor is `\`**, delete the backslash and insert a newline instead of submitting.

Octo_pi's editor does not implement this fallback.

## Upstream code reference

`tmp/pi-mono/packages/tui/src/components/editor.ts:725-731, 739-745`:

```ts
// In the tui.input.submit branch:
const currentLine = this.state.lines[this.state.cursorLine] || "";
if (this.state.cursorCol > 0 && currentLine[this.state.cursorCol - 1] === "\\") {
  this.handleBackspace();
  this.addNewLine();
  return;
}
this.submitValue();
```

The same logic is also reachable via the `tui.input.newLine` branch (lines 716-732) using a helper `shouldSubmitOnBackslashEnter`. Two paths converge on identical behavior.

## Files to modify

- The Elixir editor handler that processes `"tui.input.submit"`. Locate via grep `"tui.input.submit"` in `apps/octo_pi_tui/lib/`. Likely lives next to the `tui.input.newLine` handler.

## Acceptance

- Typing `foo\<Enter>` produces a multi-line buffer `foo\n` (the `\` is consumed) and does **not** submit.
- Typing `foo<Enter>` (without trailing backslash) still submits normally.
- Typing `foo \ <Enter>` (backslash with trailing space) **submits** (because the char immediately before cursor is space, not `\`).
- Test covers all three cases.

## Blast radius

Editor-internal. No registry change. Tiny.
